Atomic variables and concurrent collections.

A full lock is the right tool for a multi-step critical section, but it's heavy for the most common case of all: one variable that several threads increment, or one collection they all read and write. For those, Java's java-util-concurrent package gives you data structures that are already thread-safe — the coordination is built into the object, so you write no locks at all. Two families cover almost everything: atomic variables, and concurrent collections.

Start with atomic variables. Atomic-integer, atomic-long, and atomic-reference each wrap a single value, and expose operations that are indivisible in hardware, not by locking. The broken counter becomes correct and fast: create an atomic-integer, and call increment-and-get. That's a single atomic step — read, add, and write, all at once — so the interleaving that lost updates simply can't happen.

How does that work without a lock? Under the hood these use a CPU instruction called compare-and-swap. The idea is: if this value is still what I last read, set it to the new value; otherwise, tell me it changed. It's optimistic — no thread blocks. A thread that gets beaten simply retries in a small loop. And for arbitrary updates, update-and-get and accumulate-and-get run your own function inside exactly that retry loop — for example, keeping a running maximum. Because nobody blocks, atomics scale better than locks under high contention. But remember: they only protect one variable. Two atomics touched together are not atomic as a pair — that still needs a lock.

Now the collections. For shared collections, don't reach for a plain hash-map — it can corrupt under concurrent writes — and don't reach for the old synchronized-map, which is correct but wraps everything in a single coarse lock. Use the purpose-built concurrent types. Concurrent-hash-map is the workhorse: highly concurrent reads and writes, with no external locking. Copy-on-write-array-list gives cheap reads and expensive writes — ideal for read-mostly lists, like event listeners. And the blocking-queue family is the backbone of producer-consumer handoff, where take blocks until an item arrives.

There's one subtle trap left. A concurrent map makes each call thread-safe, but a check-then-act across two calls is still a race. If you write, if the map does not contain the key, then put the key — two threads can both pass the contains check before either one puts. The fix is to use the map's atomic compound methods, which do the check and the act as a single operation. Compute-if-absent computes and puts exactly once. And merge is perfect for a concurrent tally — merge word, one, integer-sum gives you a thread-safe word count in one line.

So the guiding rule for this whole section: prefer these ready-made tools over hand-rolled locks. An atomic-integer or a concurrent-hash-map is easier to get right than a synchronized block, faster under load, and far harder to deadlock. Drop to explicit locks only when your invariant spans several variables at once. One piece of the puzzle remains, though — not atomicity, but visibility. How does a thread even know that a write happened? That's the memory model, and it's next.
